<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>app.curant.autoupdate</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Homebrew python3.12, NOT /usr/bin/python3 (Apple's system
             Python) - same reasoning as every other scheduled job here:
             this run may send a text via osascript/Messages.app to tell
             the customer an update happened (or failed), and macOS ties
             Automation/AppleEvents permission to the exact requesting
             binary. Apple's system python3 was never granted that
             permission; the Homebrew one already was. -->
        <string>/opt/homebrew/opt/python@3.12/bin/python3.12</string>
        <string>-u</string>
        <string>/usr/local/bin/curant-watcher.py</string>
        <string>--auto-update-check</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Checks every 30 minutes (explicit "check every 30 minutes"
         request, 2026-08-18) - previously a fixed 4am off-peak slot.
         Real tradeoff, not hidden: curant-cli auto-update is a no-op
         unless a genuinely newer version is actually available AND
         config["auto_update_enabled"] is true (default on - see
         `curant-cli set-auto-update`), so most of these firings cost
         nothing beyond one small network check - but on the rare
         occasion an update IS applied, it can now land at any time of
         day, including mid-conversation, rather than only at a chosen
         off-peak hour. Low real risk in practice: conversation state
         lives in chat.db/local sqlite, not in the watcher process's
         memory, so a restart between poll cycles doesn't lose anything
         - but it's still a real behavior change from the original
         design, worth knowing. Same pattern already used elsewhere in
         this file (see com.curant.meetingprep.plist's 900s / 15-minute
         StartInterval, com.curant.irisinbox.plist's 3600s / hourly). -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>1800</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- launchd does not source .zshrc/.zprofile, so curant-cli
         (installed to this Mac's personal ~/bin) is not found on the
         default PATH. Without this, curant-watcher.py's own internal
         subprocess.run(["curant-cli", "auto-update"]) call fails
         outright with "No such file or directory", same bug already
         confirmed live and fixed in every other plist here. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/AbhyudayDhundhel/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <!-- Dev/testing bypass, matching every other plist here.
             Remove once a real license is activated via curant-cli
             activate. -->
        <key>CURANT_DEV_UNLICENSED</key>
        <string>1</string>
    </dict>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/curant-autoupdate.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/curant-autoupdate-error.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
<!--
Install with:
  cp com.curant.autoupdate.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
  launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.curant.autoupdate.plist

On by default for every customer (config["auto_update_enabled"] defaults
to True; a customer can turn it off with `curant-cli set-auto-update off`,
or run updates by hand only via `curant-cli update`/`curant-cli
check-update`). install.command generates this plist with the real
detected $HOME/$PY312 paths, same as every other scheduled job - never
install this checked-in copy directly, since it hardcodes the original
developer's username and an Apple-Silicon-only Homebrew path.

To test immediately rather than waiting for 4am, run curant-watcher.py
directly with the flag shown in ProgramArguments above.

Logs land at /tmp/curant-autoupdate.log and /tmp/curant-autoupdate-error.log.
-->
